incorporated in 2007 and is based in Orlando, United States.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Marcel  Verbaas | Chairman of the Board & CEO | Age 55 | Compensation: $2.8M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 42 people. Revenue per employee of $25.7M ranks in the top quartile among Real Estate peers. Each employee generates $2.1M of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $311K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 0\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags low compensation governance risk (1/10).","financial_summary":["Xenia Hotels & Resorts, Inc. is a mid-cap real estate business with a $2.2B market cap on $1.1B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +3% over the past three years, which puts it in mature, slow-growth territory.","Returns on capital run at 4% against a 8% cost of capital, which means the business is destroying value with every dollar it reinvests.","The business throws off $90M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 8 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 2.80\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, debt is heavy at 5.4\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and leverage like that is what turns a bad year into a permanent loss, and thin 1.2\u00d7 interest coverage leaves no room for a bad year.","Management returns a meaningful 5.6% of market cap to owners through dividends and buybacks, a strong signal of disciplined capital allocation, and insider ownership is negligible, so management has little personal stake in the long-term outcome."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Altman Z in the distress zone (1.0) \u2014 the kind of balance-sheet risk that can make a loss permanent","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Payout ratio looks stretched \u2014 the dividend\u2019s own margin of safety is thin","sev":"red","cat":"dividend"},{"text":"Low reinve